"use strict";(self.webpackChunkparth_panchal_github_io=self.webpackChunkparth_panchal_github_io||[]).push([[27],{8027:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"th":{"title":"Peripheral Vision","posts":[{"title":"Every Four Years, a Different Me","url":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com/p/every-four-years-a-different-me","date":"2026-06-10T13:02:27.000Z","subtitle":"Measuring my life in FIFA World Cups","image":"https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/3174b9a0-b606-4651-afe3-5c18e9490732_1920x1080.png"},{"title":"The Murder Machine da Vinci Called Pure","url":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com/p/the-murder-machine-da-vinci-called","date":"2026-05-26T15:14:56.000Z","subtitle":"On ermines, Renaissance symbolism, and the art of looking innocent","image":"https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/6f7727d1-0074-4a30-a3bf-c827161020f0_1920x1080.webp"},{"title":"Meet the AI That\'s Going to Secure the Internet","url":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com/p/meet-the-ai-thats-going-to-secure","date":"2026-04-11T15:49:32.000Z","subtitle":"It was leaked. Then leaked again. Then formally announced.","image":"https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/d308eece-8dc1-4b02-9d9e-be26b8936562_2048x1075.webp"},{"title":"Some Things Are Worth Fighting For","url":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com/p/some-things-are-worth-fighting-for","date":"2026-02-07T16:01:32.000Z","subtitle":"Film vs digital, the format that almost died, and why people are coming back","image":"https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/a9e9cb00-bd11-425d-b9e7-bfdaa90b9446_750x500.jpeg"},{"title":"Starting from the edges...","url":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com/p/starting-from-the-edges","date":"2025-10-10T14:45:33.000Z","subtitle":"Okay, we\'re officially doing this. You ready? Let\'s go.","image":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1453738773917-9c3eff1db985?crop=entropy&cs=tinysrgb&fit=max&fm=jpg&ixid=M3wzMDAzMzh8MHwxfHNlYXJjaHwzOHx8cmFuZG9tfGVufDB8fHx8MTc2MDA0NDAxMHww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=80&w=1080"}],"home":"https://parthhpanchal.substack.com"},"WL":{"user":"parthpanchal","profile":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/","films":[{"title":"Hum Tum","year":"2004","rating":4.5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-07-28","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/4/9/4/3/7/49437-hum-tum-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=9b5024172f","review":["I watched this so many times as a kid that my parents recorded it on VHS. I\u2019d dance in front of the TV to Ladki Kyun and Chak De without understanding a single thing about what the movie was actually saying. Rewatching it now, I finally got it.","The animated sequences are the key to everything. Karan is a cartoonist and Hum and Tum are his creation, his way of reducing men and women to comfortable stereotypes from a safe distance. The cartoon is where he\u2019s most honest, and most wrong. At the start Hum and Tum bicker endlessly, reducing each other to types. That\u2019s exactly how Karan sees Rhea when they first meet. Not as a person, as a category. A strong, opinionated woman who won\u2019t fall for his charm. She\u2019s material for the strip before she\u2019s anything else to him.","What Kohli does brilliantly is let the animated sequences evolve as Karan does. Every time Hum and Tum appear, they\u2019re a little different. The bickering gets softer. The caricatures gain something resembling feeling. Karan doesn\u2019t realise he\u2019s falling for Rhea before his own cartoon does. The strip is his emotional diary and it gives him away long before he\u2019s ready to admit anything.","That\u2019s what makes the hate-to-love work here. It\u2019s not a switch that flips, it\u2019s a slow erosion. Saif plays Karan with this cocky lightness that gradually cracks open. Rani plays Rhea as someone guarded not out of coldness but out of loss, a widow who already knows what it feels like to have love taken away. Her reluctance isn\u2019t a romantic device, it\u2019s earned.","By the end Hum and Tum aren\u2019t fighting anymore. And neither are Karan and Rhea. The cartoon was never about men vs women. It was about one man slowly realizing he\u2019d been drawing the wrong story all along.","I was dancing to Ladki Kyun as a child without knowing why it made me feel something. Now I do."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/hum-tum/"},{"title":"People We Meet on Vacation","year":"2026","rating":4,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-07-20","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/9/3/2/8/5/6/932856-people-we-meet-on-vacation-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=c98853a2fc","review":["Every vacation in this film is a time capsule. Brett Haley uses the non-linear structure brilliantly, jumping between summers to show you not just where Poppy and Alex have been, but who they were to each other at each point. The vacations aren\u2019t just settings, they\u2019re emotional checkpoints. Each one tells you exactly how close they are to admitting something neither of them wants to say out loud.","What the film is really about is home. Not a place, a person. Poppy is restless, spontaneous, always chasing the next thing. Alex is grounded, routine-driven, someone who knows exactly where he belongs. They shouldn\u2019t work as friends and they absolutely do. Ten years of shared summers will do that. The tragedy underneath all of it is that Poppy has been running toward new experiences her whole life without realising she keeps coming back to the same person. Alex has always been her home. She just couldn\u2019t see it until she almost lost it.","Tom Blyth and Emily Bader have the kind of chemistry that feels lived in rather than performed. The banter, the inside jokes, the way they move around each other. You believe ten years of this. Keegan DeWitt\u2019s soundtrack sits perfectly underneath it all, warm and nostalgic without being saccharine.","The film understands something most rom-coms miss. The most terrifying thing isn\u2019t falling in love with a stranger, it\u2019s realising you\u2019ve already fallen for someone you can\u2019t afford to lose. Poppy and Alex don\u2019t need to find each other. They just need to stop pretending they haven\u2019t already."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/people-we-meet-on-vacation/"},{"title":"Disclosure Day","year":"2026","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-06-18","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/5/9/2/5/1/1159251-disclosure-day-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=9ae810e4bb","review":["Spielberg has made alien movies before but this one feels different. Less interested in the spectacle of contact, more interested in what happens after. What would the truth actually do to us? Would it unite us or fracture us further? What happens to faith when we discover there are other supreme beings out there?","The film works as a metaphor for empathy but what hit me hardest was the anthropocentricity running through it. We\u2019ve built entire civilizations on the assumption that everything around us exists to serve us. Disclosure Day quietly dismantles that. The biblical character names scattered throughout aren\u2019t accidental. Spielberg is interested in what we worship, what we fear, and whether those two things are different at all.","Janusz Kaminski\u2019s camera is almost never still, constantly spinning around characters, breaking expected axes of action, keeping you unsteady. Several oners land beautifully, rarely showy but breathtaking when they hit. Then there\u2019s Williams, their 30th collaboration, and Spielberg described this score perfectly: music written not to lead the film but to sit underneath it, giving it a slight nudge forward. It works exactly like that.","Josh O\u2019Connor, Emily Blunt, Colman Domingo. Everyone is excellent. Some themes get stated too plainly when the images already said it. But even a slightly blunt Spielberg is still Spielberg.","At 79 this feels less like revisiting familiar territory and more like saying everything he\u2019s been building toward. At some point every Spielberg alien film becomes a film about us. This one more than most."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/disclosure-day/"},{"title":"Backrooms","year":"2026","rating":4,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-28","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/9/7/6/4/1/9/976419-backrooms-2026-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=f7a99e3fc8","review":["There\u2019s something deeply unsettling about liminal spaces before you even add a monster. The wrongness is baked in. Empty corridors, fluorescent lighting that hums too loud, yellow walls that go on forever. The feeling of being somewhere you\u2019re not supposed to be. Backrooms understands this and then makes it so much worse.","Parsons takes the three most primitive human fears, being alone, fear of the unknown, and the feeling of being followed, and escalates all three relentlessly. Each sequence ups the ante. There were moments where I was genuinely holding my breath without realising it. Deathly creepy in a way that gets under your skin and stays there.","Ejiofor carries the film, restrained and terrified in exactly the right measure. Parsons built 30,000 square feet of practical sets so the cast was actually walking those corridors. The crew apparently got lost on set. That commitment makes the dread feel real.","This started as a single image on 4chan, became a free YouTube series made by a teenager with Blender, and is now an A24 film. Kane Parsons, Curry Barker, the Philippou brothers. YouTubers are just making the most exciting horror right now and I love that for us."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/backrooms-2026/"},{"title":"Obsession","year":"2025","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-05-14","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/2/3/4/4/7/2/1234472-obsession-2025-2-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=cff6fc00b6","review":["Obsession feels like a new wave of horror that\u2019s plainly uncomfortable to watch and that might be the best kind. Curry Barker takes the monkey\u2019s paw trope and makes insane use of it. Bear wishes for Nikki to love him more than anything and gets exactly that. Watching everything unravel is something to study.","Inde Navarrette puts on the performance of her career. She goes from lively and confident to this anxious, pleading presence in seconds. The way she contorts her face, screams at things only she can see, her entire body language shifts. Unhinged and brilliant. I can see her making big moves after this.","What makes this work is how it understands obsession as poison. Nikki becomes a shadow of herself, robbed of agency, reduced to a shell that exists only to please Bear. The cruelest part is she\u2019s still punished for actions that aren\u2019t her own. The film explores toxic relationships through manipulation and fixation without being preachy.","Barker\u2019s direction is creative. The use of focus pulling to create separation and split scenes is done beautifully. The film asks an uncomfortable question: do you recognize yourself in Bear or Nikki? That\u2019s what makes it disturbing. It\u2019s about romantic entitlement, consent, and mistaking intensity for connection.","This is horror that implicates you instead of just scaring you. Uncomfortable, unnerving, genuinely fresh."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/obsession-2025/"},{"title":"Dhurandhar The Revenge","year":"2026","rating":3,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-21","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/4/5/4/1/5/1/1454151-dhurandhar-the-revenge-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=567766a7ed","review":["Entertaining but nowhere near the first one. Part 1 had this balance where the spy thriller actually worked as a thriller. Part 2 just doubles down on everything without the restraint. Nearly four hours of Ranveer finally getting to do more, but the runtime kills the momentum.","The action is bigger and way more violent. Aditya Dhar knows how to stage these sequences and the sound design hits hard. Ranveer as Hamza gets to actually lead this time instead of being sidelined, and he\'s good when the script lets him be. The problem is the film forgets it\'s supposed to be tense espionage and just becomes a revenge fantasy.","The political messaging that was subtle in the first one is completely blatant here. It stops being subtext and becomes the text. Makes it hard to just enjoy the craft when the film keeps reminding you what it wants you to think.","First Dhurandhar was smart and restrained. This one\'s just louder."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/dhurandhar-the-revenge/"},{"title":"Project Hail Mary","year":"2026","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-29","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/6/1/1/2/8/8/611288-project-hail-mary-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=ac31b6ec03","review":["Project Hail Mary showed up at the exact moment we needed it. Optimism feels radical right now, and this movie is drenched in it.","Gosling plays Ryland Grace like someone who accidentally became important and hates every second of it until he doesn\'t. Grace isn\'t saving the world because he\'s brave, he\'s doing it because Rocky\'s there and quitting would mean letting his friend down. That\u2019s it. That\'s the whole movie.","Speaking of, Rocky is suchhh a vibe. A rock alien with no face who communicates through musical tones becomes the heart of the film. They used a practical puppet on set instead of CGI and you can tell. The chemistry between Gosling and a literal rock is real.","Greig Fraser shot the entire thing practically. No green screen anywhere. The spaceship set was fully built. Fraser pulled infrared filters from ARRI Alexa 65 cameras, rigged glass panes with water funnels for that wet feeling in certain shots, built cages with blinking infrared lights that created pink bokeh on camera. All practical. When Gosling\'s floating in zero gravity, he\'s actually hung on wires in a massive set. The space sequences feel tactile because they are.","Lord and Miller made this feel loose even though it\'s meticulously crafted. There\'s a karaoke scene in a movie about saving humanity from extinction. It works. The film arrives when the world feels divided beyond repair and shows two beings who can barely communicate finding a way to work together anyway.","Never thought I\'d sit in a theater full of people crying over a rock alien. But here we are. \ud83e\udd72"],"spoilers":true,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/project-hail-mary/"},{"title":"Crime 101","year":"2026","rating":3.5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-03-16","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/0/5/8/1/3/2/1058132-crime-101-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=eaba4b3224","review":["Loved this way more than I thought I would. There\'s something about crime dramas set in LA that just hit different. The way Bart Layton shoots the city at night, those overhead shots of the 101 freeway, the inverted skyline establishing shots. Reminded me of Drive immediately.","Chris Hemsworth playing a methodical jewel thief is great casting. He\'s not doing the usual charisma thing, he\'s just calm and careful. Mark Ruffalo as the detective closing in on him brings the weight. Their cat and mouse dynamic works because both of them feel worn out by their lives. Barry Keoghan shows up as the wildcard and completely steals every scene he\'s in.","Erik Wilson\'s cinematography is stunning. The way he shoots LA, those sweeping nighttime vistas, the way the camera tilts and rotates over the freeway creating these surreal perspectives. Blanck Mass did the score and it\'s all pulsating synth that just sits under everything perfectly. The opening heist sequence is shot with this clinical precision that pulls you in immediately.","Everyone\'s comparing it to Heat and yeah, the influence is obvious. But it earns the comparison. Layton knows how to build tension and the film never feels like it\'s trying too hard to be something it\'s not. Just a solid, sleek crime thriller that understands what makes these LA noir films work."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/crime-101/"},{"title":"Dune: Part Two","year":"2024","rating":4.5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-18","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/6/1/7/4/4/3/617443-dune-part-two-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=cc533700f8","review":["Paul riding the sandworm for the first time is still one of the most perfect sequences I\'ve seen. The way Villeneuve builds to it, the sound design when Paul finally stands up on it, the score kicking in. Pure cinema. Then he immediately uses that moment to start his war and it all turns dark fast.","The Harkonnen planet sequence is fucked up in the best way. Everything\'s black and white, completely lifeless, and Butler just goes full psycho in that arena. Austin Butler doing whatever the hell that voice is as Feyd-Rautha shouldn\'t work but it does. He\'s unhinged. The knife fight with Paul at the end is tense as hell even though you know how it ends.","Paul\'s arc is what makes it work. He\'s not some reluctant hero, he makes a choice. He sees the nightmare coming and drinks the water anyway because revenge matters more than anything else. By the time he\'s won you\'re supposed to feel gross about it. The holy war starts and it should make your stomach drop, not make you cheer.","This is what Part One was setting up. Messiah is going to be insane."],"spoilers":true,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/dune-part-two/1/"},{"title":"Dune","year":"2021","rating":4.5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-03-17","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/sm/upload/nx/8b/vs/gc/cDbNAY0KM84cxXhmj8f0dLWza3t-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=49eed12751","review":["The Part 3 trailer dropped and I had to go back. Watching this again, what stands out is how Villeneuve refuses to give you wonder. Greig Fraser shoots Arrakis like a graveyard. Wide frames, negative space, characters dwarfed by brutalist architecture. The camera just sits and stares instead of cutting away. It\'s oppressive by design.","The visual austerity is the point. The Atreides see Arrakis as raw material, not a living world. Villeneuve gives us their colonizer perspective and makes us sit in that discomfort. Even moments that could\'ve been spectacle immediately turn tense. Zimmer\'s score does the same thing, bagpipes for imperial House Atreides, throat singing turned into atmospheric dread for the Fremen.","Chalamet plays Paul with almost no affect and it works because he\'s being shaped into a weapon. Ferguson carries the emotional weight. The film ends right when Paul is accepted by the Fremen, right before he rides a sandworm. Not a cliffhanger, a warning.","Into the desert we go."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/dune-2021/3/"},{"title":"The Revenant","year":"2015","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-02-27","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/2/0/7/2/2/4/207224-the-revenant-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=f51b30a589","review":["First time watching this since 2016. My dad and I used to watch all the Oscar Best Picture nominees every night before my board exams back in India. Saw this on our laptop between cramming sessions. Seeing it in IMAX ten years later hit completely different.","Lubezki and I\xf1\xe1rritu shot everything on natural light with the Alexa 65. No artificial lighting, just whatever the sun gave them. Shots sit and breathe instead of cutting away. The pacing is slow in the best way possible. Glass isn\u2019t some hero getting revenge, he\u2019s barely alive and refuses to die because fuck Fitzgerald.","The wide-angle lenses on faces let you see Glass\u2019s eyes up close while still catching how the wilderness is crushing him. Those long sweeping shots during fights instead of quick cuts, plus that massive 2.76:1 aspect ratio, make you feel like you\u2019re moving with the camera. You\u2019re stuck in it with him. DiCaprio just endures. Every breath, every crawl through snow, every moment of agony feels real because the camera never looks away.","Nature doesn\u2019t care about Glass\u2019s pain or his dead son or his need for vengeance. The wilderness just exists. That indifference makes his survival feel less like triumph and more like he\u2019s too stubborn to quit. What keeps him going isn\u2019t hope or love, it\u2019s pure rage. Maybe that\u2019s more honest than most survival films admit.","Still feels like nothing else."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/the-revenant-2015/1/"},{"title":"Wuthering Heights","year":"2026","rating":4,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-17","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/9/6/9/1/1/1196911-wuthering-heights-2026-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=7d02cf3bdc","review":["Emerald Fennell made a Gothic romance for people who thought Saltburn wasn\'t horny enough.","This isn\'t your English class Wuthering Heights. Fennell takes the obsessive, destructive mess at the heart of Bront\xeb\'s novel and just goes for it. No apologies. The whole thing feels like it\'s about to combust at any moment, and honestly? I was into it.","Robbie and Elordi have this unhinged chemistry that makes you feel like you\'re intruding on something private. They\'re playing people who would burn everything down just to feel something, and I bought it completely. There\'s a rawness to how they interact that feels dangerous.","What got me is how Fennell uses all the excess to say something real. The heightened sexuality, the over-the-top Gothic aesthetic, even the Charli xcx songs\u2014it all feels like the inside of someone\'s head when they\'re completely consumed by another person. Cathy and Heathcliff don\'t know how to be normal about each other because nothing about them was ever normal to begin with.","This film gets that obsession isn\'t romantic. It\'s poison. These two mistake intensity for love and destroy everyone in their path. Watching them implode is messy and uncomfortable and kind of brilliant.","It\'s indulgent as hell and knows exactly what it\'s doing. Some people will absolutely hate that. I couldn\'t look away."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/wuthering-heights-2026/"},{"title":"A Year in The Life at MKBHD.","year":"2026","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-10","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/4/9/3/9/2/8/1493928-a-year-in-the-life-at-mkbhd-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=4f4580325c","review":["Long live longform content! \u2764\ufe0f"],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/a-year-in-the-life-at-mkbhd/"},{"title":"Sentimental Value","year":"2025","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-02-02","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/0/1/3/3/5/7/1013357-sentimental-value-2025-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=e89e64a309","review":["Watched this on my bed and didn\'t cry once throughout the entire film. Then the credits rolled and I completely broke.","The performances are stunning. Reinsve, Skarsg\xe5rd, Lilleaas, Fanning\u2014everyone gets it.","There\'s something about people who can only express love through their work. Art becomes the safe distance. They\'ll pour everything into a film or performance, but ask them to just sit with their kid and they have nothing.","What Trier understands is that forgiveness isn\'t clean. You don\'t have one conversation and decades of absence suddenly make sense. You just learn to exist in the same space with all that unresolved feeling. Sometimes the people who hurt us most genuinely thought they were doing their best. That doesn\'t make it okay, but it makes it harder to hate them.","Maybe I cried at the credits because the film was finally over and I could stop holding everything in. Or maybe it\'s because Trier made something that understood some pain doesn\'t have a resolution, it just becomes part of who you are.","2025 Ranked"],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/sentimental-value-2025/"},{"title":"Coherence","year":"2013","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-31","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/sm/upload/kp/ay/8h/oo/sDhWq6zSWW95Po5HJCeH0NVyblY-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=a286174883","review":["They don\u2019t make them like this anymore"],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/coherence/1/"},{"title":"Dhurandhar","year":"2025","rating":4,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-29","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/7/4/5/8/2/1174582-dhurandhar-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=179b5169d5","review":["One last movie with The Boys \u2122\ufe0f Also fuck Netflix for that weird ass color palette, keep it to your own content ffs \ud83d\udd95 Apart from that, Shashwat Sachdev is the goat and I can see him getting big in the near future. His music weaves the movie together so well"],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/dhurandhar/1/"},{"title":"Sinners","year":"2025","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-25","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/1/6/6/0/0/1116600-sinners-2025-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=5996b7d555","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/sinners-2025/4/"},{"title":"Everything Everywhere All at Once","year":"2022","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2025-11-23","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/4/7/4/4/7/4/474474-everything-everywhere-all-at-once-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=281f1a041e","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/everything-everywhere-all-at-once/3/"},{"title":"Everything Everywhere All at Once","year":"2022","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":true,"watched":"2026-01-24","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/4/7/4/4/7/4/474474-everything-everywhere-all-at-once-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=281f1a041e","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/everything-everywhere-all-at-once/2/"},{"title":"28 Years Later: The Bone Temple","year":"2026","rating":3.5,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-22","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/5/6/4/0/9/1156409-28-years-later-the-bone-temple-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=131058c6ff","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/28-years-later-the-bone-temple/"},{"title":"Happy Patel: Khatarnak Jasoos","year":"2026","rating":3.5,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-21","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/6/6/2/7/0/1166270-happy-patel-khatarnak-jasoos-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=97198ae102","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/happy-patel-khatarnak-jasoos/"},{"title":"Hamnet","year":"2025","rating":4,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-08","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/7/7/2/2/3/2/772232-hamnet-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=631489314d","review":["Had to let this one sit with me for a few days. Hamnet is a pleasing and a hard watch at the same time, and that contradiction is what makes it so powerful.","The pleasing part is pure craft. Chloe Zhao directs with such precision and purpose. Working with cinematographer Lukasz Zal, every frame is intentionally composed: rule of thirds, leading lines, frames within frames, visual repetition. The camera is mostly stationary, often wide, allowing the compositions to breathe. The natural lighting through the forest, the way she uses negative space, the symmetry in the domestic scenes contrasted with the chaos of grief. You could study this frame by frame for composition alone.","Then comes the hard part. The part that destroys you. Jessie Buckley and Jacobi Jupe broke me completely. Watching Agnes lose her son, the way grief doesn\'t announce itself but slowly suffocates everything. These performances are devastating. Buckley embodies the kind of loss that hollows you out from the inside. And Jupe as Hamnet brings such vulnerability and life to a boy we know is doomed from the start, which makes every moment with him unbearable. Everyone in my screening was in tears.","Zhao crafted something beautiful and brutal in equal measure. The technical brilliance makes the emotional devastation hit even harder. You can admire the craft while it tears your heart out. That\'s the contradiction that makes Hamnet unforgettable."],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/hamnet/"},{"title":"Stranger Things 5: The Finale","year":"2025","rating":null,"liked":false,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2025-12-31","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/4/4/0/3/3/4/1440334-stranger-things-5-the-finale-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=b9fef563bd","review":null,"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/stranger-things-5-the-finale/"},{"title":"Marty Supreme","year":"2025","rating":5,"liked":true,"rewatch":false,"watched":"2026-01-02","poster":"https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/1/1/9/7/4/9/9/1197499-marty-supreme-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=b14a26bb43","review":["Timoth\xe9e Chalamet plays the ultimate prick to perfection. Marty Mauser is despicable, manipulative, and an asshole to everyone around him. And somehow, impossibly, you\'re rooting for him to win.","This is the best use of the \\"obsessed artist\\" trope in years. Marty doesn\'t just want to be great at table tennis, he needs it like oxygen. Josh Safdie understands that true obsession isn\'t romantic, it\'s destructive and selfish. Marty robs his uncle, abandons his pregnant girlfriend, uses people like stepping stones, all in service of proving he\'s the best. The film doesn\'t apologize for him. It just shows you what ambition looks like when it consumes everything else.","What makes this different from something like Whiplash or Black Swan is that Marty isn\'t tortured by his pursuit. He\'s intoxicated by it. No self-doubt, no internal conflict. He\'s a hustler who found the one thing he\'s genuinely exceptional at, and he\'s going to ride that talent as far as it\'ll take him.","The pacing is relentless. Safdie shoots this like Uncut Gems but with pure velocity instead of anxiety. The film moves so fast you can\'t sit back. Darius Khondji\'s 35mm cinematography on vintage anamorphic lenses gives it tactile 1950s authenticity with modern frenetic energy.","The music drives everything. Daniel Lopatin\'s score combined with period song choices creates this intoxicating rhythm. The soundtrack doesn\'t just support the film, it possesses it.","Chalamet is mesmerizing. He commits so completely to Marty\'s narcissism and charm that you understand why people keep getting pulled into his orbit even as he destroys them. All swagger with desperation underneath.","By the end, you\'ve watched someone burn every bridge to cross one finish line. And you never looked away.","Timoth\xe9e Chalamet playing an irredeemable prick shouldn\'t work this well. But here we are. Go get your Oscar.","2025 Ranked"],"spoilers":false,"url":"https://letterboxd.com/parthpanchal/film/marty-supreme/"}]}}')}}]);